Falsifies `kernel::arch::x86_64::ist::tests::every_online_core_has_its_own_three_interrupt_stack_table_entries`.

Architecture: x86_64

The defect: `ist::install` points each core's IST entries at the next core's stacks rather than its
own, and writes its per-CPU pointer into them. Every entry is nonzero and mapped, so nothing
faults; two cores whose NMIs or machine checks overlap would share a stack, and a core would read
another core's per-CPU pointer in the paranoid entry. The patch is a one-character index slip.

Expected to fail: "cpu 0: vector 8's IST entry ... is not its own stack's".
Replayed red on patagonia, 2026-10-13 UTC, `script/test --arch x86_64 --no-host --test every_online_core_has_its_own_three_interrupt_stack_table_entries`
(QEMU q35 under PVH, two cores); green with the fix.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/ist.rs b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/ist.rs
index 85f631331..3da0e3a5b 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/ist.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/ist.rs
@@ -152,7 +152,7 @@ fn region() -> (u64, u64) {
 /// core's own per-CPU block.
 pub unsafe fn install(id: usize, percpu: u64) {
     for k in 0..VECTORS.len() {
-        let ist_top = span(id, k).1 - TOP_RESERVE;
+        let ist_top = span((id + 1) % MAX_CPUS, k).1 - TOP_RESERVE;
         // SAFETY: `ist_top` is 16 bytes below the top of this core's own stack `k`, inside the slot,
         // 8-aligned, and nothing has run on the stack yet (no IST vector can be delivered before
         // the IDT this core loads after this call).
